   "source": [
    "#Adding a totally random variable to check the value of r and rsquare\n",
    "# Add a useless random variable\n",
    "np.random.seed(123)  # different seed for new randomness\n",
    "data[\"RandomNoise\"] = np.random.normal(0, 1, n)\n",
    "# Define predictors including the useless variable\n",
    "X_extended = data[[\"StudyHours\", \"SleepHours\", \"Attendance\", \"RandomNoise\"]]\n",
    "# Add constant\n",
    "X_extended = sm.add_constant(X_extended)\n",
    "# Fit model\n",
    "model_extended = sm.OLS(y, X_extended).fit()\n",
    "# Summary\n",
    "print(model_extended.summary())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "cfd15bb4-7347-4010-9d2e-3a3fb94046ac",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# After introducing a purely random variable (RandomNoise) into the model, the R-squared increased from 0.709 to 0.716. This confirms a fundamental property of R-squared: it never decreases when additional predictors are included, regardless of their relevance.\n",
    "# However, the Adjusted R-squared increased only marginally (0.700 to 0.705), reflecting the penalty imposed for additional model complexity. Since the sample size is relatively large (n = 100), the penalty effect is limited for a single added variable.\n",
    "# The coefficient associated with RandomNoise is not statistically significant (p = 0.112), indicating that it does not meaningfully contribute to explaining variation in Score. This demonstrates that statistical significance testing is essential for identifying irrelevant predictors.\n",
    "# Overall, this experiment highlights why relying solely on R-squared can be misleading and underscores the importance of Adjusted R-squared and hypothesis testing in regression modeling."
   ]
  },
